WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:05.279
As the first true standalone story in the
Star Wars saga, there was a whole lot riding

00:00:05.279 --> 00:00:06.370
on Rogue One.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:09.830
But the story surrounding the film is almost
as interesting as the one we watched on the

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:10.830
big screen.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:15.099
From axed cameos to the film's humble beginnings,
there's a whole lot more to Rogue One than

00:00:15.099 --> 00:00:17.520
just Jyn's quest to snag the Death Star plans.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.840
So let's dig into the untold truth of Rogue
One.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:22.620
Unexpected origins

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:27.570
The entire concept for Rogue One came from
two sentences in A New Hope's opening crawl.

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:31.440
But it was a visual effects supervisor for
the special editions who had the bright idea

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.879
to turn those lines into a full-fledged movie.

00:00:33.879 --> 00:00:38.149
FX guru John Knoll came up with the pitch
when Lucasfilm wanted to do a live action

00:00:38.149 --> 00:00:40.859
Star Wars series, but the idea was shelved.

00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:45.090
When Disney bought the franchise and announced
new plans to do standalone stories, Knoll

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.589
ran the idea past his colleagues, and a blockbuster
was born.

00:00:48.589 --> 00:00:51.449
So at least something good came of those special
editions.

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:54.140
(Blaster fire)

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:58.350
And despite the fact that the entire movie
is based on an opening crawl, it doesn't have

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:00.809
one itself, leaping right into the action.

00:01:00.809 --> 00:01:05.269
But it turns out the first draft of the film
actually did have a crawl — until director

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.640
Gareth Edwards decided to remove it.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:11.930
That's because Rogue One delves into the battle
referenced in the introduction to A New Hope.

00:01:11.930 --> 00:01:15.850
The creative team worried it would be a bit
redundant to have an opening crawl for events

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.570
already recapped in another one.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.560
A collage of other movies

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:23.650
The film's editors revealed that the film
was sketched out with an unusual approach.

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.610
Instead of working off a script, they cut
footage from hundreds of other movies to follow

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:32.240
the scene-by-scene story breakdown in order
to figure out exactly how much dialogue the

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:33.469
film would actually need.

00:01:33.469 --> 00:01:37.189
This approach also helped them figure out
how long different sequences would take so

00:01:37.189 --> 00:01:39.420
they could hash out a more detailed script.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:43.359
On the surface, it feels like a backwards
way to make a movie, but it worked out pretty

00:01:43.359 --> 00:01:44.359
well.

00:01:44.359 --> 00:01:46.649
Now we'd just like to get our hands on that
mock-up reel…

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:47.929
Trailer tribulations

00:01:47.929 --> 00:01:52.950
Rogue One's reshoots have been reported exhaustively,
with scene-by-scene breakdowns of everything

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.210
that was left out of the final cut of the
film, and it's now obvious entire subplots

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.000
would end up on the cutting room floor.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.939
Why did all those early scenes remain in the
trailers in the weeks and months leading up

00:02:02.939 --> 00:02:06.520
to release, long after it was obvious they'd
never be in the final cut?

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:10.790
According to Edwards, it was a decision made
on the marketing side, because the folks cutting

00:02:10.790 --> 00:02:15.240
those commercials and trailers felt they did
a good job of selling the "spirit" of the

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.360
film, regardless of the final film's story.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.000
Now we'll never know if Baze and Chirrut were
supposed to kiss.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Oh baby.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.980
Alan Tudyk, and his cut cameo

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.500
This one gets a little meta, so just bear
with us.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:32.280
Alan Tudyk played the fan favorite droid K-2SO,
and wore a motion capture rig to bring him

00:02:32.280 --> 00:02:33.280
to life.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.040
But he also popped up as a pilot in a scene
that didn't make it into the final film.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.040
The weird part?

00:02:38.040 --> 00:02:42.200
Tudyk played the cameo role as Wray Nerely,
from his hit web series Con Man, where he

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.480
plays a self-deprecating sci-fi actor bouncing
between fan conventions.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.620
Wray never has much luck keeping big roles,
and it seems even his real-life Rogue One

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:53.740
cameo couldn't survive that fictional streak
of bad breaks.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.640
"Did you know that wasn't me?"

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:02.650
"Heh."

00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:07.020
In terms of the performance Tudyk was actually
hired to portray, the snarky droid is around

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:12.231
seven feet tall, and even though K-2SO was
created with motion capture and CGI, they

00:03:12.231 --> 00:03:16.300
still needed Tudyk to be around the droid's
approximate height while acting with the rest

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.300
of the cast.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:21.550
The solution was to put Tudyk on stilts, which
he wore for much of the production.

00:03:21.550 --> 00:03:26.000
It might sound like a hassle, but it sure
beats having to wear an entire C-3PO costume,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.470
like Anthony Daniels did.

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:30.530
When the two actors met, Daniels had some
choice words for Tudyk.

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:36.349
"He said 'Wait a minute, are you wearing an
actual robot costume or are they doing it

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:37.349
motion capture?'

00:03:37.349 --> 00:03:39.750
And I said 'Motion capture.'

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.190
And he said 'You ----!'"

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.769
Everything old is new again

00:03:43.769 --> 00:03:48.650
Edwards raided the vaults at Skywalker Ranch
and Lucasfilm and discovered unused footage

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:50.330
from the first Star Wars film.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:54.209
Keen-eyed fans noticed some familiar faces
during Rogue One's final battle.

00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:58.659
That's because Edwards painstakingly restored
some unused alternate shots of the pilots

00:03:58.659 --> 00:04:01.690
during the Rebels' attack on the Death Star
in A New Hope.

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.629
It's a nod for fans, but it's also one more
subtle way that Rogue One enriches the far-away

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:07.200
galaxy we know and love.

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.530
That wasn't the only genuinely vintage thing
about the film, either.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:14.800
To get the film to sync up with the look of
A New Hope, Edwards used the vintage Ultra

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.160
Panavision 70mm lenses Quentin Tarantino unearthed
at Panavision for the filming of The Hateful

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:24.160
Eight, and attached them to an ultra high-tech
6k large format camera.

00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:28.500
It proved the perfect combination of lo-fi
and high tech, and allowed Edwards to strike

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:32.030
the same balance that made the original trilogy
look so memorable.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:33.340
A new composer

00:04:33.340 --> 00:04:37.270
Along with being the first standalone Star
Wars film, Rogue One also marked the end of

00:04:37.270 --> 00:04:42.120
an era for franchise composer John Williams,
who has scored every film in the series over

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:43.120
the decades.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:47.690
This time around, Edwards opted to bring in
Michael Giacchino — who's scored films like

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:51.919
The Incredibles, the new Star Trek franchise,
and Doctor Strange — to provide the soundtrack

00:04:51.919 --> 00:04:53.070
to Jyn's adventure.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:57.639
Giacchino adds enough musical tributes that
it still "feels" like Star Wars, while also

00:04:57.639 --> 00:04:59.150
setting a different tone.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.860
Meanwhile, Williams is set to return for Episodes
8 and 9, so we'll get more old-school Star

00:05:03.860 --> 00:05:05.639
Wars themes pretty soon.

00:05:05.639 --> 00:05:07.120
Peter Jackson was there

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.430
He might be the mind behind one of the biggest
genre franchises in history, but even Peter

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:14.210
Jackson has to tip his hat when Darth Vader's
around.

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.690
The man behind the Lord of the Rings and Hobbit
trilogies happened to be in town when Edwards

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:23.120
was directing that epic closing scene when
Darth Vader goes berserk on escaping Rebel

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:26.150
agents, so Edwards gave him a heads up for
when he was filming.

00:05:26.150 --> 00:05:31.039
Jackson ran over immediately to geek out over
Vader mowing through a hallway of soldiers.

00:05:31.039 --> 00:05:35.770
Famous director or not, no one can blame Jackson
for going full-on fanboy and basking in Vader's

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:36.390
glory.

00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:41.640
"That's awesome!"

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:43.680
"Ugh."

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.140
"That sounds so cool!"

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.480
Thanks for watching!

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:52.420
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.740
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

